/* ---- chapter 12 ---- */
N('12:1','a woman wrapped in the sun','views',{t:'Who is the woman?', p:'Israel, Mary, the church, or all three?', b:`<b>Israel</b>: the sun, moon and twelve stars come from Joseph's dream of his family (Genesis 37:9), and Israel "gives birth" to the Messiah (Isaiah 66:7–8; Micah 5:3). <b>Mary</b>: she literally bore the child (a common Catholic and Orthodox reading). <b>The church</b>: her other children are believers (12:17). Many readers see the faithful people of God across both covenants.`, r:'Genesis 37:9; Isaiah 66:7–8; Micah 5:3; 12:17', top:'woman-of-ch12', s:['koester-rev','gregg-four-views']});
N('12:1','a crown of twelve stars','myth',{t:'A queen of heaven', p:'Goddesses were pictured crowned with stars. So was the European flag.', b:`Goddesses were honored as queens of heaven. The clearest parallel is Isis: the Latin novelist Apuleius describes her appearing with a disk like the moon on her brow and a dark cloak sprinkled with glittering stars and a half moon (<i>Metamorphoses</i> 11.3–4, second century AD). John's woman is the true queen-mother. The circle of twelve gold stars on the European flag (1955) was partly inspired, by its designer's later account, by images of Mary based on this verse; the Council of Europe's official explanation says only that twelve stands for completeness and unity.`, l:[['Isis','Isis'],['Flag of Europe','Flag of Europe']], top:'woman-of-ch12'});
N('12:3','a great fiery-red dragon with seven heads','myth',{t:'The seven-headed dragon', p:'Canaanite texts describe Litan, "the mighty one with seven heads."', b:`At Ugarit (about 1300 BC) Baal defeats Litan, "the twisting serpent, the mighty one with seven heads." The Old Testament calls this monster Leviathan and says God crushed its heads (Psalm 74:13–14; Isaiah 27:1). Babylon's Tiamat and Greek Typhon are similar chaos monsters.`, r:'Psalm 74:13–14; Isaiah 27:1; Job 41:1', l:[['Lotan','Lotan'],['Leviathan','Leviathan'],['Chaoskampf','Chaoskampf']], top:'dragon', s:['collins-combat']});
N('12:4','a third of the stars','views',{t:'A third of the angels fell?', p:'A popular belief, but the text may mean something else.', b:`Many Christians believe a third of the angels followed Satan in a rebellion before creation, based on this verse. They also point to 12:7–9, where the dragon has "his angels," who are thrown down with him, and many commentators (futurist and some idealist) do read the stars of 12:4 as those angels. On the other side, the verse is placed at the birth of Christ, and it echoes Daniel 8:10, where a horn (Antiochus) throws down some of the stars, meaning God's people. The idea of a pre-creation fall comes more from later tradition (and Milton's <i>Paradise Lost</i>) than from this text.`, r:'Daniel 8:10; Isaiah 14:12–15; Ezekiel 28:12–17; Jude 6', top:'dragon'});
N('12:5','snatched up to God','word',{w:'rapture', p:'The book’s only "rapture" (harpazō) is Jesus’s own ascension.', top:'rapture'});
N('12:5','shepherd all the nations with an iron rod','echo',{t:'The shepherd with the iron rod', p:'Psalm 2:9 in its Greek form, already given to the faithful in 2:27.', b:`The line is Psalm 2:9, the coronation psalm of God's anointed king, as the Greek Old Testament reads it: "you will shepherd them" (<i>poimaineis</i>). The Hebrew, read with different vowels, says "you will crush them with an iron rod" (the site's Psalm 2:9). The note at 2:27 explains the two readings. Here the promise belongs first to the child himself; 19:15 uses it again for the rider on the white horse.`, r:'Psalm 2:9; 2:27; 19:15', top:'messiah'});
N('12:5','She gave birth to a son','myth',{t:'Leto, Python and Apollo', p:'In Greek myth a dragon hunted a pregnant goddess. Ephesus told this story.', b:`The dragon Python hunted the pregnant Leto to keep her from bearing Apollo. She escaped (helped by the north wind or Poseidon) and gave birth on an island. Apollo later killed Python. Ephesus claimed the birth happened nearby, and Roman emperors presented themselves as Apollo. Many scholars think John retells the myth with Jesus as the real dragon-slayer.`, l:[['Leto','Leto'],['Python (mythology)','Python (mythology)']], top:'other-traditions', s:['collins-combat','koester-rev']});
N('12:7','Michael','echo',{t:'Michael', p:'In Daniel, the great prince who stands guard over Israel.', b:`In Daniel 10:13, 21 and 12:1 Michael is Israel's great prince, the one who stands over Daniel's people and rises up in a time of distress like no other. Jude 9 calls him the archangel. In Revelation the war is fought in heaven, but 12:11 says the real victory came "because of the blood of the Lamb" (12:11).`, r:'Daniel 10:13; Daniel 12:1; Jude 9; 12:11', top:'michael'});
N('12:9','the ancient serpent','echo',{t:'The serpent of Eden', p:'The dragon is the snake of Genesis 3, finally exposed.', b:`Revelation names the serpent of Genesis 3 as the devil, a link the Old Testament never makes outright (compare Wisdom of Solomon 2:24, Scripture for Catholics and Orthodox, and Romans 16:20). Genesis 3:15 promised that the woman's offspring would strike the serpent on the head; chapter 12 shows a woman, a child, and the serpent. 12:17 keeps the link: "the rest of her offspring" uses <i>sperma</i>, the word the Greek Old Testament uses for the woman's offspring in Genesis 3:15.`, r:'Genesis 3:1; Genesis 3:15; Romans 16:20; 20:2', top:'dragon'});
N('12:10','the accuser','word',{t:'Satan means "accuser"', greek:'ὁ κατήγωρ (ho katēgōr)', p:'The Hebrew satan is a prosecutor, as in Job and Zechariah.', b:`In Job 1–2 and Zechariah 3:1, "the satan" is the accuser in God's court. <i>Diabolos</i> (devil) means "slanderer." John's Greek word <i>katēgōr</i> is a legal term borrowed into Hebrew and Aramaic as well. After Christ's victory, the prosecutor loses his place in court (compare Romans 8:33–34).`, r:'Job 1:6–12; Zechariah 3:1–2; Romans 8:33–34; Luke 10:18'});
N('12:11','They won the victory over him because of the blood of the Lamb','spec',{t:'Two weapons and a willingness', p:'The war in heaven is won on earth, by a cross and by testimony.', b:`Verses 7–9 show an angelic war; verses 10–11 reveal how it was really won: because of the Lamb's blood, because of believers' testimony, and through their refusal to love their lives (on "because of" or "by," see the word note on this verse). My reading: Michael's battle is the heavenly side of the cross, which explains why it happens right after the child is "snatched up" (12:5). Jesus links his death with Satan being "thrown out" in John 12:31–32.`, r:'John 12:31–32; Colossians 2:15; 12:5', top:'overcomer'});
N('12:11','because of the blood of the Lamb','word',{t:'"Because of" or "by" the blood?', greek:'διὰ τὸ αἷμα ... διὰ τὸν λόγον (dia to haima ... dia ton logon)', kind:'deb', p:'With this case, dia usually gives the reason, not the tool.', b:`<i>Dia</i> followed by the accusative case normally means "because of" or "on account of." Many English Bibles say "by the blood of the Lamb," as if the blood and the testimony were the weapons used. The grammar points first to the reason: the believers won because the Lamb had died and because they held to their testimony, even to death (Aune and Koester read it this way). "By" is still defensible, since a cause can also be a means, and the two senses meet at the cross. The Plain follows the usual meaning of the grammar.`, r:'5:9; 1:5; 7:14', top:'overcomer', s:['aune-rev','koester-rev']});
N('12:14','two wings of the great eagle','echo',{t:'On eagles’ wings', p:'God said he lifted Israel up "on the wings of eagles."', b:`Exodus 19:4: "I lifted you up on the wings of eagles and brought you to me." Deuteronomy 32:11 pictures God as an eagle spreading its wings over its young. The woman's flight into the wilderness, where she is fed, retells Israel's Exodus and manna. Some preterists see the Jerusalem church fleeing to Pella before AD 70 (Eusebius 3.5.3).`, r:'Exodus 19:4; Deuteronomy 32:11; Isaiah 40:31; Exodus 16:35', top:'exodus-pattern'});
N('12:14','a time, and times, and half a time','echo',{t:'Daniel’s time formula', p:'Daniel 7:25 and 12:7: three and a half "times."', b:`The phrase comes from Daniel 7:25 and Daniel 12:7. Here it equals the 1,260 days of 12:6, which shows that John treats the different phrases as one period.`, r:'Daniel 7:25; Daniel 12:7; 12:6', top:'three-and-a-half'});
N('12:16','The earth opened its mouth','echo',{t:'The earth swallows', p:'In the Exodus story, the earth swallowed Pharaoh’s army and later Korah.', b:`Exodus 15:12 says of Pharaoh's army at the sea: "the earth swallowed them." Numbers 16:32: "the earth opened its mouth and swallowed" Korah's rebels. Creation itself takes the side of God's people.`, r:'Exodus 15:12; Numbers 16:32', top:'exodus-pattern'});
N('12:17','the rest of her offspring','views',{t:'Who are "the rest of her offspring"?', p:'The church on earth, a future remnant, or (for Adventists) a last-days remnant church?', b:`The woman's son is safe with God; her other children stay on earth and bear the dragon's attack. <b>Most readers</b>, ancient and modern, see the whole church after Christ's ascension, made up of all who keep God's commandments and hold to Jesus' testimony. <b>Many futurists</b> see believers, often Jewish believers, in the last three and a half years. <b>Seventh-day Adventists</b> read the KJV's "remnant of her seed" as the last-days remnant church: those who keep all of God's commandments, the Sabbath included, and who have "the testimony of Jesus," which 19:10 calls the spirit of prophecy. Adventist teaching sees that gift at work in the ministry of Ellen G. White, and identifies the Seventh-day Adventist Church with this remnant, while holding that God has faithful people in every church (Fundamental Beliefs 13 and 18). Other Christians do not accept that identification.`, r:'19:10; 14:12; Genesis 3:15; 1:2', top:'remnant', s:['sda-beliefs','white-controversy','koester-rev']});
N('12:17','the testimony of Jesus','word',{t:'The testimony "of Jesus"', greek:'τὴν μαρτυρίαν Ἰησοῦ (tēn martyrian Iēsou)', kind:'deb', p:'The testimony about Jesus, or the testimony Jesus himself gave?', b:`The Greek simply says "the testimony of Jesus," and the phrase can mean two things. It can be the testimony believers give <i>about</i> Jesus (many translations), or the testimony Jesus himself gave as the faithful witness of 1:5, which believers hold on to and pass on (1:2, 9). Many commentators think John means both. The same question comes back at 14:12 ("the faith of Jesus") and 19:10 and 20:4. The Plain keeps the open Greek here; for Adventist readings of this verse, see the note on "the rest of her offspring."`, r:'1:2; 1:5; 1:9; 19:10; 20:4; 14:12', s:['koester-rev','bauckham-theology']});

/* ---- chapter 13 ---- */
N('13:1','a beast coming up out of the sea','echo',{t:'Daniel’s four beasts in one', p:'Leopard, bear and lion, the beasts of Daniel 7, combined.', b:`Daniel 7:3–7 sees four beasts rise from the sea: a lion, a bear, a leopard, and a terrible fourth beast with ten horns. John's beast combines all four, in reverse order. Daniel's beasts are kingdoms (7:17). In John's time, the empire combining all earlier empires was Rome.`, r:'Daniel 7:2–8; Daniel 7:17; Daniel 7:23–24', top:'beasts'});
N('13:2','The beast I saw','word',{t:'Thērion: a wild animal', greek:'θηρίον (thērion)', p:'Not the "living creatures" of chapter 4. A wild, hunting animal.', b:`<i>Thērion</i> is a wild or dangerous animal, used for animals in the arena where Christians were killed. The word appears 39 times in Revelation: 38 times for the beasts of chapters 11–20, and once for the wild animals of 6:8. The heavenly creatures of chapter 4 are <i>zōa</i>, "living ones."`, top:'beasts'});
N('13:3','its death wound was healed','history',{t:'The Nero legend', p:'Rumors said Nero had not died, or would return from the dead.', b:`Nero killed himself in AD 68 with a sword wound to the throat. Rumors spread that he was alive in the East and would return; at least three impostors appeared (Tacitus, <i>Histories</i> 2.8; Suetonius, <i>Nero</i> 57). Others link the healed head to Rome surviving the chaos of AD 69. Futurists expect a future ruler who survives or fakes a fatal wound.`, l:[['Nero redivivus legend','Nero redivivus legend']], top:'nero', s:['koester-rev','aune-rev']});
N('13:4','Who is like the beast?','spec',{t:'A counterfeit of "Who is like God?"', p:'The people’s cry parodies Israel’s song, and the name Michael.', b:`"Who is like you among the gods, O LORD?" was Israel's song after the Red Sea (Exodus 15:11). The name Michael means "Who is like God?" Many scholars note the parody of Exodus 15. My addition: placed just after Michael's victory in chapter 12, the crowd's question "Who is like the beast?" answers Michael's own name with the wrong answer.`, r:'Exodus 15:11; Micah 7:18; 12:7', top:'unholy-trinity'});
N('13:5','42 months','symbol',{t:'Limited authority', p:'"Was given," "was allowed": the beast’s power is on loan, for a set time.', b:`The verb "was given" appears again and again in chapter 13 (verses 5, 7, 14, 15). The beast's authority comes from the dragon (13:2), but the time limit comes from God. Forty-two months is three and a half years, Daniel's "a time, times, and half a time" for the little horn's rule (Daniel 7:25; Daniel 12:7), and the length of the drought in Elijah's day as Jesus and James tell it (Luke 4:25; James 5:17), which lies behind the two witnesses of 11:6. Some also see an echo of Israel's 42 camps in the wilderness (Numbers 33).`, r:'Daniel 7:25; Daniel 12:7; Luke 4:25; James 5:17; 11:2–6; Numbers 33:1–49', top:'three-and-a-half'});
N('13:7','make war on the saints','echo',{t:'Daniel\'s little horn', p:'Daniel 7:21: the horn made war with the holy ones and overpowered them.', b:`The words come from Daniel 7:21, where the little horn on the fourth beast "made war with the holy ones" and prevailed against them, until the Ancient of Days came and the holy ones received the kingdom (Daniel 7:22). With 13:1–2, it marks the sea beast as Daniel's fourth beast and its little horn. Daniel's vision also gives the ending: the time is set, and the kingdom goes to the holy ones. Some old manuscripts lack this sentence; see the variant.`, r:'Daniel 7:21–22; Daniel 7:25; 11:7', top:'daniel'});
N('13:8','since the world was made','word',{t:'What happened "since the world was made"?', greek:'ἀπὸ καταβολῆς κόσμου (apo katabolēs kosmou)', p:'Names written since creation, or the Lamb slain since creation?', b:`In the Greek, the phrase comes right after "the Lamb who was slaughtered," so word order favors "the Lamb slain from the foundation of the world" (KJV, NIV): the cross planned before creation (compare 1 Peter 1:19–20). But 17:8 uses the same phrase with names "written in the book of life since the world was made," and no Lamb, which favors "names written from the foundation of the world" (ESV, NRSV). The Plain follows 17:8 against the word order. The verse is also cited in the debate over election (compare 3:5; Ephesians 1:4).`, r:'17:8; 1 Peter 1:19–20; Ephesians 1:4', top:'book-of-life'});
N('13:11','another beast coming up out of the earth','views',{t:'The second beast', p:'Later called "the false prophet." It looks like a lamb, but speaks like a dragon.', b:`Readers have seen: the priests of the imperial cult in Asia (preterists, many scholars); the papacy (Reformers); a future religious leader (futurists); or any religion that serves state power (idealists). From the 1850s, Seventh-day Adventist writers identified it with the United States, a "lamb-like" young nation with two horns (civil and religious liberty).`, top:'false-prophet', s:['friesen-cults','gregg-four-views']});
N('13:10','into captivity they go','echo',{t:'Jeremiah\'s captivity and sword', p:'A saying from Jeremiah 15:2 and 43:11, turned from judgment into a call to endure.', b:`Jeremiah 15:2 lists the fates God has assigned to faithless Judah: those meant for death, to death; those meant for the sword, to the sword; those meant for captivity, to captivity (compare Jeremiah 43:11). John borrows the pattern, but here the people facing captivity and the sword are the saints. The point is not that they deserve it, but that the beast's power is limited and allowed (13:5, 7), and that endurance, not fighting back, is their part. The manuscripts differ in both halves of the saying; see the variants.`, r:'Jeremiah 15:2; Jeremiah 43:11; Matthew 26:52; 13:7', top:'martyrs'});
N('13:14','came back to life','word',{t:'"Came back to life"', greek:'ἔζησεν (ezēsen)', kind:'nu', p:'The same verb Revelation uses for Jesus rising from the dead.', b:`The Greek says only that the beast "lived," in the past tense: it had the sword wound "and lived." Revelation uses the same verb for Jesus, the one who died and came back to life (2:8), and for the martyrs who come to life in 20:4. The head of the beast also looked slaughtered (13:3), the word used of the Lamb in 5:6. The beast is a counterfeit Lamb, with its own death and return to life; 17:8 adds that it once was, now is not, and will come again. English Bibles that say "and yet lived" hide the parody.`, r:'2:8; 20:4; 5:6; 13:3; 17:8', top:'unholy-trinity'});
N('13:15','give breath to the beast\'s image','history',{t:'Speaking statues', p:'Ancient temples had statues that seemed to speak, and priests who staged wonders.', b:`Ancient writers describe hollow statues that "spoke" and tricks that made fire appear. Lucian (2nd century) mocked the false prophet Alexander, who made a snake-god appear to speak. In Asia, cities competed to build imperial temples with large emperor statues.`, l:[['Alexander of Abonoteichus','Alexander of Abonoteichus']], top:'imperial-cult', s:['friesen-cults','aune-rev']});
N('13:16','a mark on their right hand or on their forehead','word',{w:'inhand', p:'The Greek says "on," not "in." Charagma was a stamp or seal.', top:'mark'});
N('13:17','nobody can buy or sell','history',{t:'Trade and the gods', p:'Guild membership and markets were tied to the worship of patron gods and the emperor.', b:`In cities like Thyatira, trade guilds held feasts honoring patron gods, and markets displayed the emperor's image. Roman coins bore his image and titles. Refusing to take part could cost a Christian their livelihood, a kind of pressure the letters of chapters 2–3 already describe.`, top:'mark', s:['koester-rev','friesen-cults']});
N('13:18','a human number','views',{t:'What is "a human number"?', p:'A name spelled in numbers? A number of imperfection? Readers have counted for 1,900 years.', b:`<b>Translation</b>: <i>arithmos anthrōpou</i> can be "the number of a man" (a particular person) or "a human number" (a number people can work out), as the angel's measure in 21:17 is a human measure. <b>Gematria</b>: Greek and Hebrew letters were also numbers, so a name had a number. "Nero Caesar" spelled in Hebrew letters, נרון קסר, adds up to 666, a spelling found in a document from Wadi Murabba'at; the Latin-style spelling without the final n gives 616, the number in some manuscripts (see the variant). <b>Irenaeus</b> (about AD 180) tried names such as Euanthas, Lateinos and Teitan, preferred Teitan, and warned against being sure (<i>Against Heresies</i> 5.30.3). <b>Idealists</b>: six falls short of seven, three times over. <b>Historicists</b> have counted papal titles, <b>futurists</b> expect a future ruler. The views are set out further on the 13:16–18 card.`, r:'21:17; 13:17; 15:2', top:'666', l:[['Number of the beast','Number of the beast'],['Gematria','Gematria']], s:['irenaeus','aune-rev','koester-rev','bauckham-climax']});

/* ---- chapter 14 ---- */
N('14:1','Mount Zion','echo',{t:'The king on Zion', p:'Psalm 2: "I myself have put my king in place on my holy mountain, on Zion."', b:`Psalm 2:6 sets God's anointed king on Zion against the rebellious nations, which is exactly the contrast with chapter 13. Joel 2:32 promises that on Mount Zion there will be survivors who escape. Hebrews 12:22 speaks of "the heavenly Jerusalem," Mount Zion, as something believers have already come to.`, r:'Psalm 2:6; Joel 2:32; Hebrews 12:22', top:'mount-zion'});
N('14:1','written on their foreheads','symbol',{t:'Two foreheads', p:'Chapter 13 marks people with the beast’s name. Chapter 14 shows God’s.', b:`The two chapters are set side by side: the beast's mark and name (13:16–17) versus the Lamb's and Father's names (14:1). The high priest wore "Holy to the LORD" on his forehead (Exodus 28:36–38). Everyone belongs to someone.`, r:'13:16–17; Exodus 28:36–38; 22:4', top:'mark'});
N('14:4','did not soil themselves with women','word',{w:'virgins', p:'A soldier’s purity before holy war? Faithfulness without idolatry?', top:'144000'});
N('14:4','firstfruits','israel',{t:'Firstfruits', p:'The first part of the harvest, given to God, promising more to come.', b:`Firstfruits were the first sheaves offered at the feast before the full harvest (Leviticus 23:10). James 1:18 calls believers firstfruits, so to speak, of the things God made. In this chapter, a full harvest follows (14:14–16).`, r:'Leviticus 23:10; James 1:18; Jeremiah 2:3; 14:15', top:'harvest'});
N('14:6','eternal good news','views',{t:'The three angels’ messages', p:'A last call to repent. Seventh-day Adventists see their mission here.', b:`The first angel calls every nation to "fear God and give him glory" and worship the Creator. Adventist tradition reads 14:6–12 as God's last message, centered on the Sabbath (Exodus 20:11). Most others read a final offer of grace to all nations before the harvest.`, r:'Exodus 20:11; Matthew 24:14; 14:7', top:'three-angels'});
N('14:8','Fallen, fallen is Babylon the Great','echo',{t:'Isaiah’s fall of Babylon', p:'"Fallen! Fallen is Babylon!" (Isaiah 21:9).', b:`The cry is from Isaiah 21:9, and "Babylon the great" from Daniel 4:30, where Nebuchadnezzar boasts of the city he built. The wine comes from Jeremiah: "A cup of gold, that was Babylon, in the hand of the LORD, getting the whole earth drunk" (Jeremiah 51:7). The Greek word for her "raging" passion, <i>thymos</i>, is also the word for God's anger in 14:10: the wine she pours becomes the wine she must drink.`, r:'Isaiah 21:9; Daniel 4:30; Jeremiah 51:7–8; 18:2', top:'babylon'});
N('14:10','mixed undiluted in the cup of his wrath','echo',{t:'The cup of God\'s wrath', p:'Strong wine, not cut with water: the prophets\' cup of judgment.', b:`Ancient wine was normally mixed with water before drinking. <i>Akratos</i> means "unmixed," full strength, so the phrase is a deliberate paradox: "mixed undiluted." The image is the prophets' cup of judgment: "the LORD holds a cup in his hand, its wine foaming, full of spiced mix" (Psalm 75:8); Jerusalem had drunk "the cup of his fury" (Isaiah 51:17); and Jeremiah was told to make the nations drink the cup of the wine of God's wrath (Jeremiah 25:15–29). "Fire and sulfur" recalls the judgment on Sodom, where "the LORD rained sulfur and fire" (Genesis 19:24), and on Edom (Isaiah 34:9).`, r:'Psalm 75:8; Isaiah 51:17; Jeremiah 25:15–29; Genesis 19:24; Isaiah 34:9; 16:19; 18:6', top:'wrath'});
N('14:11','The smoke of their torment goes up forever and ever','views',{t:'Eternal torment?', p:'Eternal conscious punishment, final destruction, or symbol?', b:`Traditional readers see eternal conscious punishment. Others (conditional immortality, or "annihilationism") note that the image is copied from Isaiah 34:10, where of Edom "its smoke will keep going up forever" (Isaiah 34:10), yet the land is then inhabited by wild animals, so the image means complete, permanent destruction. A third group stresses that the scene is a warning meant to lead to repentance (14:7, 12). The churches' own answers, which really do conflict, are set side by side in the "Where Christians differ" card at chapter 20 (20:10, 14–15).`, r:'Isaiah 34:9–10; Genesis 19:28; 19:3; 20:10', top:'lake-of-fire', s:['koester-rev','gorman-reading']});
N('14:12','the faith of Jesus','word',{t:'"The faith of Jesus"', greek:'τὴν πίστιν Ἰησοῦ (tēn pistin Iēsou)', kind:'deb', p:'Faith in Jesus, faithfulness to Jesus, or Jesus\' own faithfulness?', b:`The Greek is as open as the English: "they keep ... the faith of Jesus." It may mean their faith in Jesus (NIV, ESV), their faithfulness to him, or the faith Jesus himself kept and taught, which they hold on to (KJV keeps "the faith of Jesus"). "Keep" suits something received and guarded, like the commandments in the same line. It is the same question as "the testimony of Jesus" at 12:17 (see the note there). Seventh-day Adventists read 14:12 with 12:17 as the mark of the last-days remnant, keeping all God's commandments, the Sabbath included.`, r:'12:17; 2:13; 1:2', top:'faith', s:['koester-rev','sda-beliefs']});
N('14:13','Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord','echo',{t:'Rest from labor', p:'The second blessing of the book, for martyrs and all believers.', b:`"Rest" answers 14:11, where the beast's worshipers have "no rest." The verse echoes Hebrews 4:9–10 on entering God's rest. It is often read at Christian funerals.`, r:'Hebrews 4:9–10; 14:11; 6:11', top:'martyrs'});
N('14:14','someone like a son of man','views',{t:'Who reaps the grain?', p:'Christ, gathering his people? Or an angel of judgment?', b:`The "son of man" on a cloud with a gold crown seems to be Christ (1:13; Daniel 7:13). The grain harvest may picture the gathering of believers (Matthew 13:30; Mark 4:29); the grape harvest that follows is judgment. Some read both harvests as judgment. Notice that an angel tells him when to reap, as Jesus said only the Father knows the hour (Mark 13:32).`, r:'Daniel 7:13; Matthew 13:30; Mark 4:29; Joel 3:13; Mark 13:32', top:'harvest'});
N('14:20','1,600 stadia','word',{t:'1,600 stadia', greek:'σταδίων χιλίων ἑξακοσίων (stadiōn chiliōn hexakosiōn)', p:'The Greek gives 1,600 stadia. Like most numbers here, it carries meaning.', b:`A stadion was about 607 feet, so 1,600 stadia is about 184 miles. The number is 40 × 40 or 4 × 4 × 100, both suggesting the whole world. Some point out it is close to the length of Israel from Tyre to the border of Egypt. Converting it to miles hides the pattern, so the Plain keeps "stadia," as most literal translations do.`, r:'Isaiah 63:1–6; Joel 3:13', top:'harvest'});

/* ---- chapter 15 ---- */
N('15:2','a sea of glass mixed with fire','echo',{t:'Standing beside the Red Sea', p:'Israel sang beside the sea after Pharaoh’s army drowned.', b:`Exodus 15: having crossed the sea, Moses and Israel sing. Here the conquerors of the beast stand beside a sea of glass and sing "the song of Moses and the song of the Lamb." The fire may suggest judgment or the pillar of fire.`, r:'Exodus 14:21–31; Exodus 15:1–18; 4:6', top:'sea-of-glass'});
N('15:3','the song of Moses, the slave of God, and the song of the Lamb','spec',{t:'A song of Moses that never mentions their own victory', p:'Exodus 15 celebrates Israel’s rescue. This song celebrates God and the nations.', b:`Exodus 15 sings of Israel's escape and the enemy drowned. Remarkably, the song in Revelation 15:3–4 never mentions the singers' victory at all. It draws on Psalm 86:9 and Jeremiah 10:7 and says that all the nations will come and worship. Richard Bauckham pointed this out (<i>The Climax of Prophecy</i>). "Slave of God" was Moses' title of honor (Joshua 14:7; Deuteronomy 34:5, Hebrew <i>ebed YHWH</i>). My addition: since the beast's followers just heard a call to repent (14:6–7), the song of the saved is about the nations turning to God, not about their own triumph.`, r:'Exodus 15:1–18; Psalm 86:9; Jeremiah 10:7; Deuteronomy 32:4; Joshua 14:7; 14:6–7', top:'worship', s:['bauckham-climax','bauckham-theology']});
N('15:5','the tent of the testimony','echo',{t:'The tent of witness', p:'The tabernacle in the wilderness, where the covenant tablets were kept.', b:`The "tent of the testimony" (Numbers 9:15; 17:7) held the ark and the tablets of the law. Judgment comes from the place of God's covenant. The smoke filling the temple recalls Exodus 40:34–35 and Isaiah 6:4.`, r:'Numbers 9:15; Exodus 40:34–35; Isaiah 6:4; 1 Kings 8:10–11', top:'temple'});

/* ---- chapter 16 ---- */
N('16:2','nasty, painful sores','echo',{t:'The bowls and the trumpets', p:'Sores, blood, darkness: the plagues of Egypt again, now complete.', b:`The bowls follow the order of the trumpets: land, sea, rivers, sun, darkness, Euphrates, storm. The trumpets struck a third; the bowls strike all. Sores were Egypt's sixth plague (Exodus 9:8–11), and they fall on those who carry the beast's mark.`, r:'Exodus 9:8–11; Exodus 7:17–21; Exodus 10:21–23; 8:7–12', top:'bowls'});
N('16:5','and who was','word',{t:'Who is, who was, and no "who is coming"', greek:'ὁ ὢν καὶ ὁ ἦν (ho ōn kai ho ēn)', kind:'nu', p:'God\'s threefold name loses its third part, because now he has come.', b:`In 1:4 and 1:8 God is the one who is, who was, and who is coming. Here, as at 11:17, the angel says only "who is and who was." Most commentators (Bauckham, Koester) take the change as deliberate: the one who was to come has now come in judgment, so the future part is no longer future. The KJV's "and shalt be" comes from a guess by the editor Theodore Beza, not from any Greek manuscript; see the variant.`, r:'1:4; 1:8; 4:8; 11:17', s:['bauckham-theology','koester-rev']});
N('16:6','you have given them blood to drink','symbol',{t:'Justice that fits', p:'They poured out blood; they receive blood.', b:`The angel calls this just because the punishment matches the crime. The pattern of "measure for measure" runs through the book: Babylon receives the cup she mixed (18:6), and the destroyers of the earth are destroyed (11:18).`, r:'18:6; 11:18; Isaiah 49:26; Galatians 6:7', top:'wrath'});
N('16:9','They did not repent and give him glory','spec',{t:'Plagues meant to turn people', p:'Twice the bowls say people did not repent. Repentance was still possible.', b:`Twice the bowls note that people did not repent (16:9, 11), just as in 9:20–21, and three times that they insulted God (16:9, 11, 21). If the plagues were only punishment, there would be no reason to note that people refused to repent. My reading: even the last judgments in Revelation are designed as a final appeal, like the plagues of Egypt, which were meant so that "Egypt will know that I am the LORD" (Exodus 7:5).`, r:'9:20–21; 16:11; 16:21; Exodus 7:5; 2 Peter 3:9', top:'repentance'});
N('16:12','the kings from where the sun rises','history',{t:'Kings from the east', p:'For John’s readers, the Parthians. For later readers, many candidates.', b:`Rome's eastern rivals the Parthians crossed the Euphrates more than once, and the Nero legend said Nero would return with them. In history and in the 20th century, readers have suggested Muslims, Japan, China, or other eastern powers. Cyrus took Babylon by diverting the Euphrates (Herodotus 1.191), and Isaiah 44:27–28 links the drying of rivers to Cyrus.`, r:'Isaiah 44:27–28; Jeremiah 51:36; 9:14', top:'euphrates'});
N('16:13','three unclean spirits like frogs','echo',{t:'Frogs', p:'Egypt’s second plague, and a symbol of unclean things.', b:`Frogs swarmed Egypt (Exodus 8:2–7), and Egypt's magicians copied the sign. The spirits are "unclean" (<i>akatharta</i>, the Gospels' word for the spirits Jesus drove out), and frogs were unclean animals (Leviticus 11:10). Some Persian texts call frogs creatures of the evil spirit Ahriman. Three spirits come from the three mouths of the dragon, the beast and the false prophet.`, r:'Exodus 8:2–7; Leviticus 11:10', top:'unholy-trinity'});
N('16:15','Look, I am coming like a thief!','spec',{t:'Why this interrupts Armageddon', p:'Right before the kings gather, Jesus speaks directly to the reader.', b:`Verse 15 breaks the scene with a beatitude about staying awake and keeping your clothes on, which echoes the warnings to Sardis (3:3–4) and Laodicea (3:18). My reading: John puts the call where you would least expect it, at the battle, because the real fight at Armageddon is about the reader staying faithful, not about military strategy.`, r:'3:3–4; 3:18; Matthew 24:43; 1 Thessalonians 5:2–6', top:'armageddon'});
N('16:16','Armageddon','word',{w:'armageddon', p:'Har Megiddo, "mountain of Megiddo." But Megiddo is a hill on a plain.', top:'armageddon'});
N('16:17','It is done!','echo',{t:'"It is done!"', greek:'Γέγονεν (gegonen)', p:'One word in Greek, spoken from the throne, as Jesus spoke one word from the cross.', b:`The voice from the throne says a single Greek word, <i>gegonen</i>, "it has happened, it is done." It is said again over the new creation in 21:6. Many readers hear an echo of Jesus' last word on the cross in John's Gospel, "It is finished" (John 19:30), though the Greek word there is a different one (<i>tetelestai</i>). What began at the cross is carried through to the end.`, r:'21:6; John 19:30; 15:1', top:'bowls'});
N('16:19','The great city','views',{t:'Which great city?', p:'Jerusalem, or Babylon/Rome? The verse names Babylon next.', b:`Revelation calls Babylon "the great city" again and again (17:18; 18:10, 16, 18, 19, 21), and the verse goes straight on to Babylon, so most readers take the city split in three to be Babylon, that is, Rome or the world's power center. But 11:8 called Jerusalem "the great city" too, the city where their Lord was crucified, and many preterists read 16:19 as Jerusalem, divided into factions during the Roman siege of AD 70, with Babylon's judgment following. Futurists often see a literal earthquake splitting a future Jerusalem (compare Zechariah 14:4).`, r:'11:8; 17:18; 18:10; Zechariah 14:4', top:'babylon', s:['koester-rev','gregg-four-views']});
N('16:21','about a talent in weight','word',{t:'"About a talent"', greek:'ὡς ταλαντιαία (hōs talantiaia)', p:'The Greek says the weight of a talent, very roughly 60 to 90 pounds.', b:`A talent was the largest weight in common use; it varied by region and period, and common estimates run from about 60 to 90 pounds. Josephus says that during the Roman siege of Jerusalem the catapults threw white stones weighing a talent, and that watchmen shouted a warning when one was coming (<i>Jewish War</i> 5.269–273). The Greek manuscripts have them shout "The son is coming"; many editors correct it to "The stone is coming," explaining it as a play on Hebrew <i>ben</i>, "son," and <i>eben</i>, "stone." Some preterists make much of the manuscript reading. Preterists see the siege as the fulfillment; others read a supernatural storm, as in Exodus 9:24 and Joshua 10:11.`, r:'Exodus 9:24; Joshua 10:11; Ezekiel 38:22', s:['josephus','gregg-four-views']});

/* ---- chapter 17 ---- */
N('17:1','the great prostitute who sits on many waters','echo',{t:'"You who live by many waters"', p:'Jeremiah said this to ancient Babylon, which sat on the Euphrates.', b:`Jeremiah 51:13: "You who dwell beside many waters, great in treasures, your end has come." Babylon sat on the Euphrates and its canals. 17:15 explains the waters as nations. The prophets often called idolatrous cities prostitutes (Isaiah 23:16–17 of Tyre; Nahum 3:4 of Nineveh; Ezekiel 16 of Jerusalem).`, r:'Jeremiah 51:13; Isaiah 23:16–17; Nahum 3:4; Ezekiel 16:15', top:'babylon'});
N('17:5','a mystery','word',{w:'mystery', p:'Is "Mystery" part of her name? The Greek allows "a name, a mystery."'});
N('17:6','drunk with the blood of the saints','history',{t:'Rome’s martyrs', p:'Nero’s persecution in AD 64 killed many Christians in Rome.', b:`Tacitus (<i>Annals</i> 15.44) describes Christians in Rome being torn by dogs, crucified, and burned as torches in Nero's gardens. Peter and Paul were remembered as martyrs there. Preterists who read Babylon as Jerusalem point instead to Matthew 23:35–37.`, r:'Matthew 23:35–37; 18:24', top:'nero', s:['koester-rev']});
N('17:9','seven mountains','history',{t:'Seven hills', p:'Every ancient reader knew which city sat on seven hills.', b:`Rome was "the city of seven hills" (Latin poets: Virgil, Horace, Ovid, Martial). A coin of Vespasian shows the goddess Roma reclining on seven hills. The angel adds that the heads are also seven kings, so the image is doing double duty.`, l:[['Seven hills of Rome','Seven hills of Rome']], top:'rome', s:['aune-rev','koester-rev']});
N('17:10','Five have fallen','views',{t:'Counting the seven kings', p:'Which emperors? The count changes the date of the book.', b:`Starting from Augustus: Augustus, Tiberius, Caligula, Claudius, Nero (five fallen), Galba or Vespasian (one is), and so on, depending on whether the three short reigns of AD 69 count. Starting from Julius Caesar shifts the list. Some say the seven are not emperors at all but seven empires (Egypt, Assyria, Babylon, Persia, Greece, Rome, and one to come), the common futurist view. Others read seven as a symbol of the full line of rulers. (The KJV starts this verse with "And there are seven kings." This site follows the verse division of the Greek editions, which end 17:9 with those words.)`, top:'dating', s:['aune-rev','gregg-four-views']});
N('17:11','an eighth','spec',{t:'An eighth who belongs to the seven', p:'Eight is the number of resurrection in early Christian thought.', b:`Early Christians called Sunday "the eighth day," the day of the resurrection (Barnabas 15:9). The Greek says only "he himself is an eighth"; "king" is understood from 17:10. The beast "was, and is not, and will be here" (17:8), a parody of God's name in 1:4, the one who is, who was, and who is coming. My speculation: the "eighth" is a counterfeit resurrection, a revived old power posing as a new beginning. Interestingly, the Greek letters of "Jesus" (Iēsous) add up to 888, which some ancient Christians noticed (Sibylline Oracles 1.324–30), compared with the beast's 666.`, r:'1:4; 13:3; 17:8', top:'666'});
N('17:14','Lord of lords and King of kings','echo',{t:'Lord of lords', p:'Deuteronomy 10:17 and Daniel 4:37 in the Greek.', b:`Deuteronomy 10:17: "the LORD your God is the God of gods and the Lord of lords." In the Greek version of Daniel 4:37, Nebuchadnezzar calls God "Lord of lords and King of kings." The title appears again on the rider in 19:16, in reverse order.`, r:'Deuteronomy 10:17; Daniel 4:37; 1 Timothy 6:15; 19:16', top:'second-coming'});
N('17:16','will hate the prostitute','spec',{t:'Empire eats itself', p:'The beast turns on the city that rode it.', b:`The beast and its allies destroy the prostitute. Evil in Revelation is self-destructive, and God "gave it to their hearts" (17:17). Preterists see Rome destroying Jerusalem; others, Rome burning in AD 64 or AD 69 (when the Capitol was burned in civil war); futurists a future betrayal. My reading: the scene shows that the economic system (the prostitute) and the political power (the beast) are allies of convenience, and the second will devour the first.`, r:'17:17; Ezekiel 23:25–29', top:'babylon'});

/* ---- chapter 18 ---- */
N('18:2','a home for demons','echo',{t:'A city of wild animals', p:'Isaiah and Jeremiah pictured fallen Babylon as a haunt of wild creatures.', b:`Isaiah 13:21–22; 34:11–14 and Jeremiah 50:39 describe Babylon and Edom becoming deserts haunted by owls, jackals and goat-demons. Ancient Babylon did become ruins, mostly abandoned by the 2nd century AD.`, r:'Isaiah 13:21–22; Isaiah 34:11–14; Jeremiah 50:39', top:'babylon'});
N('18:4','Come out of her, my people','echo',{t:'Leave Babylon', p:'Jeremiah 51:45: "Come out of her, my people! Let each one save his life."', b:`Jeremiah 51:45 and Isaiah 48:20; 52:11 call the exiles out of Babylon. Paul uses similar words for separation from idolatry (2 Corinthians 6:17). The reason here is to avoid sharing her sins, which suggests a spiritual and economic leaving, not only a physical one.`, r:'Jeremiah 51:45; Isaiah 52:11; 2 Corinthians 6:17; Genesis 19:15', top:'babylon'});
N('18:7','I sit as a queen','echo',{t:'"I will never sit as a widow"', p:'The boast of ancient Babylon in Isaiah 47.', b:`Isaiah 47:7–9: "You said, 'I will be the lady forever, for all time'... I will never sit as a widow... In an instant, on one day, the two will both come to you." Revelation copies the boast and the "one day" (18:8).`, r:'Isaiah 47:7–9; 18:8', top:'babylon'});
N('18:9','The kings of the earth','echo',{t:'The laments over Tyre', p:'Chapter 18 is modeled on Ezekiel’s funeral song for Tyre.', b:`Ezekiel 26–28 laments the trading city of Tyre: kings, merchants and sailors mourn, throw dust on their heads, and ask, "Who is like Tyre?" (Ezekiel 27:32). Ezekiel 27:12–24 lists Tyre's cargo, like 18:12–13 here.`, r:'Ezekiel 26:16–18; Ezekiel 27:12–36', top:'babylon'});
N('18:13','bodies, and human souls','word',{t:'"Bodies and souls of men"', greek:'σωμάτων, καὶ ψυχὰς ἀνθρώπων (sōmatōn, kai psychas anthrōpōn)', p:'Slave traders called slaves "bodies." John adds: they are human souls.', b:`In Greek commerce, <i>sōmata</i> ("bodies") was the standard word for slaves. The list runs in one grammatical form up to "bodies," then John breaks the pattern and adds "and human souls," echoing Ezekiel 27:13, where Tyre's traders deal in <i>nefesh adam</i>, literally "human souls." Some translations join the two ("slaves, that is, human lives"); the Greek simply adds the second phrase with "and." The cargo list ends with the item that exposes the rest. Rome's economy ran on slavery, perhaps 10 to 20 percent of the population.`, r:'Ezekiel 27:13; 1 Timothy 1:10', top:'babylon', s:['bauckham-theology']});
N('18:21','a stone like a huge millstone','echo',{t:'A stone thrown into the river', p:'Jeremiah had a scroll tied to a stone and thrown into the Euphrates.', b:`Jeremiah 51:63–64: "tie a stone on it and throw it into the middle of the Euphrates... Like this Babylon will sink and will not come up." Jesus spoke of a millstone hung from the neck of anyone who trips up one of the little ones (Matthew 18:6).`, r:'Jeremiah 51:63–64; Matthew 18:6', top:'babylon'});
N('18:23','The voice of bridegroom and bride','spec',{t:'The bride moves to another city', p:'The wedding voices leave Babylon and are heard again at the Lamb’s wedding.', b:`Jeremiah used this line for Jerusalem's ruin (Jeremiah 7:34; 25:10). In the very next chapter, the wedding of the Lamb is announced (19:7). My observation: the book takes the bride and the wedding music away from Babylon, and moves them to the new city, which is itself "made ready like a bride" (21:2). It is a tale of two cities, and of two women.`, r:'Jeremiah 7:34; Jeremiah 25:10; 19:7; 21:2', top:'bride'});

/* ---- chapter 19 ---- */
N('19:1','Hallelujah','word',{t:'Hallelujah', greek:'ἁλληλουϊά (hallēlouia), Hebrew "praise Yah"', p:'Its only uses in the New Testament are here, four times.', b:`<i>Hallelu-yah</i> means "Praise the LORD." It occurs four times in 19:1–6 and nowhere else in the New Testament. Psalms 113–118 (the Hallel) were sung at Passover, and Psalm 104:35 ends with the first hallelujah of the psalter, right after a prayer that sinners vanish from the earth.`, r:'Psalm 104:35; Psalm 113:1; Psalm 146:1', top:'hallelujah'});
N('19:7','the wedding of the Lamb','echo',{t:'God’s marriage to his people', p:'The prophets called Israel God’s bride. Jesus called himself the bridegroom.', b:`Isaiah 54:5: "For your husband is your Maker." In Hosea 2:19–20 God says to his people, "I will pledge you to myself in marriage forever." Jesus called himself the bridegroom (Mark 2:19–20) and told wedding banquet parables (Matthew 22:1–14; 25:1–13). Paul applies it to Christ and the church (Ephesians 5:25–32).`, r:'Isaiah 54:5; Hosea 2:19–20; Mark 2:19–20; Matthew 22:1–14; Ephesians 5:25–32', top:'bride'});
N('19:8','the right deeds of the saints','word',{w:'deeds', p:'"Righteousness of saints" is plural: righteous acts.'});
N('19:10','Worship God!','spec',{t:'Even John got it wrong, twice', p:'The book’s visionary bows to an angel, twice. A warning about being dazzled.', b:`John falls before the angel here and again in 22:8–9. The one who saw the vision was overwhelmed by its messenger. Several scholars suggest it warns against angel worship (compare Colossians 2:18). My application: if John himself could misplace his awe, readers should be humble about their interpretations, and keep worship on God alone, not on the prophecy or its interpreters.`, r:'22:8–9; Colossians 2:18; Acts 10:25–26'});
N('19:10','the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy','word',{t:'"The testimony of Jesus"', greek:'ἡ μαρτυρία Ἰησοῦ (hē martyria Iēsou)', kind:'deb', p:'Jesus\'s own witness, or witness about Jesus? The Greek allows both.', b:`The Greek "testimony of Jesus" can mean the testimony Jesus himself gave (he is "the faithful witness," 1:5), or the testimony his followers give about him. Many English Bibles pick one ("the testimony about Jesus"). This translation keeps "of Jesus," as the Greek has it, here and in 12:17 and 20:4. Richard Bauckham and Craig Koester read it mainly as Jesus's own witness, which the Spirit carries on through Christian prophets: what Jesus testified is what moves every true prophet. Others read it as witness about Jesus, so that all true prophecy points to him. Many readers think John means both, since the church's witness repeats his. Either way the angel's point holds: he and John stand on the same footing, fellow slaves who hold that witness, so worship belongs to God alone.`, r:'1:2; 1:5; 1:9; 12:17; 20:4; 22:9', top:'prophecy', s:['bauckham-theology','koester-rev']});
N('19:11','a white horse','echo',{t:'The faithful rider', p:'Christ’s white horse answers the conqueror of 6:2.', b:`In a Roman triumph the victorious general rode through the city in a chariot drawn by four horses, white ones on the grandest occasions; white horses meant victory. The rider's eyes and sword repeat 1:14–16. He judges "in righteousness," as the king does in Isaiah 11:4.`, r:'6:2; 1:14–16; Isaiah 11:4; Psalm 45:3–5', top:'second-coming'});
N('19:13','a robe dipped in blood','spec',{t:'Whose blood?', p:'The robe is bloodied before the battle starts. Three readings, and mine.', b:`Isaiah 63:1–3 pictures the LORD coming from Edom in red clothes, stained from treading the winepress: "Their juice spattered on my clothes" (Isaiah 63:3). Readers take the blood three ways. <b>The enemies' blood</b>, as in Isaiah 63: the rider comes as judge (David Aune and Grant Osborne, among others). <b>The martyrs' blood</b>: some link the robe to the winepress of 14:20 and see the blood of Christ's faithful witnesses, whom he now vindicates. <b>His own blood</b>: the robe is already dipped before any fighting is described, the armies behind him are in clean white, and elsewhere in the book the blood linked with Christ is always his own (Richard Bauckham and others). I lean to the third: the Word of God conquers as the Lamb who was slaughtered (5:6), and the only weapon used is the sword of his mouth (19:15, 21). Some manuscripts read "sprinkled" for "dipped"; see the variant.`, r:'Isaiah 63:1–3; 5:6; 7:14; 14:20; 19:15; 19:21', top:'word-of-god', s:['bauckham-theology','aune-rev','osborne-rev']});
N('19:15','he will shepherd them with an iron rod','echo',{t:'Shepherding with an iron rod', p:'Psalm 2:9, the third time Revelation uses it (2:27; 12:5).', b:`The Hebrew of Psalm 2:9 reads "You will crush them with an iron rod" (Psalm 2:9). The Greek translation of the Psalm read the same Hebrew letters as "you will shepherd them," and Revelation follows that Greek here, as in 2:27 and 12:5. A shepherd's rod guards the flock and breaks the wolves. The Psalm's king is now the rider, and his only weapon is the sword from his mouth (Isaiah 11:4).`, r:'Psalm 2:9; 2:27; 12:5; Isaiah 11:4', top:'messiah'});
N('19:17','the great supper of God','echo',{t:'Two suppers', p:'Ezekiel 39: a feast for birds over Gog’s armies.', b:`In Ezekiel 39:17–20 God tells the prophet to call every kind of bird and wild animal to gather from all around to the sacrifice he is slaughtering for them, a feast on the flesh of warriors and princes. Revelation sets this "supper of God" against the "wedding supper of the Lamb" (19:9). Every reader is invited to one of the two meals.`, r:'Ezekiel 39:17–20; 19:9', top:'gog-magog'});
N('19:20','the lake of fire','history',{t:'Gehenna and the lake of fire', p:'Jesus spoke of Gehenna, a valley outside Jerusalem.', b:`The "lake of fire" is unique to Revelation. It draws on the fire of Sodom (Genesis 19:24), Isaiah 66:24, Daniel 7:11 (the beast handed over to be burned with fire), and Jesus's Gehenna, the Valley of Hinnom where children had been sacrificed (Jeremiah 7:31). 1 Enoch 90:24–26 has fallen angels thrown into a fiery abyss.`, r:'Daniel 7:11; Isaiah 66:24; Jeremiah 7:31; Matthew 25:41; 20:10', top:'lake-of-fire'});

/* ---- chapter 20 ---- */
N('20:2','a thousand years','views',{t:'The millennium', p:'A literal future reign, the church age, or a golden age before Christ returns?', b:`<b>Premillennial</b>: Christ returns first and reigns 1,000 years on earth. <b>Amillennial</b>: the thousand years is the present church age, with Satan bound by Christ's victory (Matthew 12:29). <b>Postmillennial</b>: the gospel will spread and bring a long golden age before Christ returns. The early church had both premillennial (Irenaeus) and non-literal (Origen, later Augustine) readers.`, r:'Matthew 12:29; Colossians 2:15; 1 Corinthians 15:23–25', top:'millennium', s:['gregg-four-views','augustine-city','irenaeus']});
N('20:2','bound him','echo',{t:'Binding the strong man', p:'Jesus said he had come to tie up the strong man.', b:`Jesus: "how could anyone break into a strong man's house and seize his goods, without first tying the strong man up?" (Matthew 12:29). Amillennialists read 20:2 as that binding, with Satan unable to "lead the nations astray" (stop the gospel) during the church age. Premillennialists note that Satan is very active in the New Testament (1 Peter 5:8), so the binding must be future. Compare Isaiah 24:21–22, where the powers on high are locked in a dungeon "and after many days they will be called to account."`, r:'Matthew 12:29; Isaiah 24:21–22; 1 Peter 5:8; Jude 6', top:'millennium'});
N('20:4','judgment was given to them','word',{t:'Who sits on the thrones, and who reigns?', greek:'κρίμα ἐδόθη αὐτοῖς ... καὶ οἵτινες οὐ προσεκύνησαν (krima edothē autois ... kai hoitines ou prosekynēsan)', kind:'deb', p:'Two small Greek phrases leave the scene more open than most English Bibles do.', b:`<b>1. "Judgment was given to them."</b> This can mean they were given authority to judge (as in 1 Corinthians 6:2 and Matthew 19:28), or that a verdict was given in their favor, as in Daniel 7:22, where judgment is given for the holy ones of the Most High and they receive the kingdom. The two can go together: vindicated, then sharing the rule. Who sits on the thrones is not said: the martyrs, the apostles, the twenty-four elders of 4:4, or all the faithful. <b>2. "And those who had not worshiped the beast."</b> The Greek joins this with "and" (<i>kai hoitines</i>). Many read one group, the beheaded, described further; this site's Plain keeps the "and," which also allows a second, wider group: everyone who refused the beast, whether killed or not. David Aune and G. K. Beale both discuss the two readings. On what "came to life" means, see the note at 20:5.`, r:'Daniel 7:9; Daniel 7:22; Daniel 7:27; Matthew 19:28; 1 Corinthians 6:2; 4:4; 3:21', top:'millennium', s:['aune-rev','beale-rev']});
N('20:4','beheaded','history',{t:'Beheaded', p:'The Greek word means "beheaded with an axe," an old Roman penalty.', b:`The Greek <i>pepelekismenōn</i> comes from <i>pelekys</i>, "axe." The axe was the old penalty of the Roman Republic; by the first century, Roman citizens condemned to death were usually beheaded with the sword. Tradition says Paul, a citizen, was beheaded in Rome. John may use the old word simply for execution by Roman authority.`, top:'martyrs'});
N('20:5','This is the first resurrection','views',{t:'The first resurrection', p:'A bodily resurrection, or new life in Christ?', b:`Premillennialists: a bodily resurrection of believers before the millennium, with the rest raised after. Amillennialists: either the new birth (John 5:24–29, where Jesus speaks of a spiritual resurrection now and a bodily one later), or the souls of martyrs entering heaven. The verb "came to life" (<i>ezēsan</i>) is the one used of Jesus's resurrection in 2:8, which supports a real coming back to life, and the same verb in 20:5 is used of the rest of the dead. Many amillennial readers (following Augustine) translate it "they lived," meaning the martyrs live and reign with Christ in heaven now. Note also that some manuscripts lack the first sentence of 20:5; see the variant.`, r:'John 5:24–29; 2:8; 1 Corinthians 15:23; 1 Thessalonians 4:16', top:'first-resurrection'});
N('20:8','Gog and Magog','echo',{t:'Gog of the land of Magog', p:'Ezekiel 38–39: an invasion from the far north after Israel is restored.', b:`In Ezekiel, Gog is the ruler of the land of Magog who invades restored Israel and is destroyed by fire from heaven (Ezekiel 38:22; 39:6). In later Jewish tradition, Gog and Magog became two peoples representing all the nations. Readers have identified them with Scythians, Goths, Huns, Mongols, Turks and Russia.`, r:'Ezekiel 38:2–6; Ezekiel 38:22; Ezekiel 39:6; Genesis 10:2', top:'gog-magog'});
N('20:10','where the beast and the false prophet are too','word',{t:'"Are" or "were thrown"?', greek:'ὅπου καὶ τὸ θηρίον καὶ ὁ ψευδοπροφήτης (hopou kai to thērion kai ho pseudoprophētēs)', kind:'deb', p:'The Greek has no verb here. English has to supply one, and the choice matters.', b:`Literally: "where also the beast and the false prophet." The KJV supplies "are," as this translation does: the two thrown in at 19:20 are still there a thousand years later. Some versions supply "had been thrown," which describes only their past arrival. The next words, "they will be tormented day and night forever and ever," are the strongest text for unending punishment, and readers who hold that view stress "are." Readers who hold conditional immortality reply that the beast and the false prophet are symbols of empire and false religion, not individual persons, and that "forever and ever" can describe a result that never ends. See "Where Christians differ" on this chapter page.`, r:'19:20; 14:11; 20:14–15; Matthew 25:41; Matthew 25:46', top:'lake-of-fire'});
N('20:12','books were opened','echo',{t:'The books', p:'From Daniel 7:10: "The court sat, and books were opened."', b:`Daniel 7:10 and 12:1–2 picture books at the final judgment. Judgment is "according to their deeds," and yet entry depends on the book of life (20:15), the Lamb's book (21:27). Works show who people are; the Lamb's book shows whose they are.`, r:'Daniel 7:10; Daniel 12:1–2; Malachi 3:16; 20:15; 21:27', top:'white-throne'});
N('20:14','Death and Hades were thrown into the lake of fire','spec',{t:'Death is thrown in first', p:'The first thing to be destroyed at the end is death.', b:`Death and Hades ride as the fourth horseman (6:8). Here they are thrown into the lake of fire. Paul: "The last enemy to be done away with is death" (1 Corinthians 15:26). My reading: the lake of fire is presented as the destruction of the powers that ruin creation, which is why it is called "the second death." Whatever one concludes about its human victims, the text's first emphasis is that death itself dies.`, r:'6:8; 1 Corinthians 15:26; 1 Corinthians 15:54–55; Isaiah 25:8', top:'lake-of-fire'});

/* ---- chapter 21 ---- */
N('21:1','a new heaven and a new earth','echo',{t:'Isaiah’s new creation', p:'"For look, I am creating new heavens and a new earth" (Isaiah 65:17).', b:`Isaiah 65:17; 66:22; 2 Peter 3:13. The Greek word for new, <i>kainos</i>, often means new in quality, renewed, rather than brand new. Debate: is this creation destroyed and replaced, or renewed (Romans 8:19–22)?`, r:'Isaiah 65:17; Isaiah 66:22; 2 Peter 3:13; Romans 8:19–22', top:'new-creation'});
N('21:1','the sea is no longer there','symbol',{t:'No more sea', p:'Sea meant chaos, the beast’s home, and the gulf between John and his churches.', b:`The sea is where the beast rose (13:1), where Babylon's trade sailed (18:17), and where the dead lay (20:13). For John on Patmos, it also separated him from his churches. No more sea means nothing left that threatens or divides.`, r:'13:1; 18:17; 20:13; Isaiah 57:20', top:'sea'});
N('21:3','God\'s tent is with human beings','word',{t:'"The tabernacle of God"', greek:'ἡ σκηνὴ τοῦ θεοῦ (hē skēnē tou theou), "the tent of God"', p:'Skēnē, "tent," echoes the Hebrew Shekinah, God’s dwelling presence.', b:`<i>Skēnē</i> means tent or tabernacle. It sounds like the Hebrew <i>shekinah</i>, God's dwelling glory, and its verb is used in John 1:14: "the Word became flesh and pitched his tent among us" (<i>eskēnōsen</i>). Leviticus 26:11–12 and Ezekiel 37:27 promise that God will dwell with his people: "I will be your God, and you will be my people" (Leviticus 26:12). On the plural "peoples" here, see the variant.`, r:'Leviticus 26:11–12; Ezekiel 37:27; John 1:14; 7:15', top:'new-jerusalem'});
N('21:4','He will wipe every tear out of their eyes','echo',{t:'No more death', p:'Isaiah 25:8: "He will swallow death up for all time."', b:`Isaiah 25:8: "The Lord GOD will wipe the tears off every face." Isaiah 65:19: "The sound of weeping and the sound of crying out will no longer be heard there." This promise was given to the multitude in 7:17, and now it is fulfilled for everyone in the city.`, r:'Isaiah 25:8; Isaiah 65:19; 7:17; 1 Corinthians 15:54'});
N('21:5','I am making all things new','echo',{t:'"Something new"', p:'Isaiah heard God promise a new thing; here it is done.', b:`Isaiah 43:19: "Look at me, about to do something new." In Isaiah the new thing is a road home from exile through the desert; here it is the whole creation. Paul uses the same idea for a person in Christ: "a new creation" (2 Corinthians 5:17). This is the first time since 1:8 that the book plainly has God himself speak.`, r:'Isaiah 43:19; Isaiah 65:17; 2 Corinthians 5:17; 1:8', top:'new-creation'});
N('21:6','To anyone who is thirsty','echo',{t:'Water without price', p:'Isaiah 55:1 invites the thirsty to come and drink for free.', b:`Isaiah 55:1: "Hey! All you who are thirsty, come to the water! And you who have no money, come, buy grain and eat!" Jesus made the same call (John 7:37). The gift is free (<i>dōrean</i>, "as a gift"), the same word as in 22:17.`, r:'Isaiah 55:1; John 7:37; John 4:10; 22:17; 7:17'});
N('21:7','he will be my son','word',{t:'"He will be my son"', greek:'αὐτὸς ἔσται μοι υἱός (autos estai moi huios)', kind:'nu', p:'The promise to David\'s heir, now given to everyone who wins the victory.', b:`The Greek is singular: "I will be his God, and he will be my son." It echoes God's promise about David's heir, "I will be a father to him, and he will be a son to me" (2 Samuel 7:14), and the king's adoption in Psalm 2:7: "You are my son." What was promised to the king is now promised to each person who wins the victory, as in the seven letters (2:26–27; 3:21). "Son" here is the heir's title, and it includes daughters: Paul quotes the same promise as "you will be my sons and daughters" (2 Corinthians 6:18). Compare Romans 8:14–17, where all God's children are heirs.`, r:'2 Samuel 7:14; Psalm 2:7; 2 Corinthians 6:18; Romans 8:14–17; 2:26–27; 3:21', top:'son-of-god'});
N('21:12','the names of the twelve tribes of the Israelites','israel',{t:'Israel and the apostles in one city', p:'The gates bear Israel’s tribes, the foundations the apostles.', b:`The city is built on both: twelve tribes on the gates (from Ezekiel 48:30–35) and twelve apostles on the foundations. Many read this as the one people of God from both covenants. Some dispensationalists see Israel and the church as distinct groups who share the same city.`, r:'Ezekiel 48:30–35; Ephesians 2:19–22; Matthew 19:28', top:'church-israel'});
N('21:16','12,000 stadia','spec',{t:'The size of the known world', p:'12,000 stadia is a symbolic number, and also a huge one.', b:`12,000 stadia (about 1,400 miles) is a symbolic number (12 × 1,000), the number of God's people multiplied. My own rough estimate: a square 1,400 miles on each side would cover most of the eastern Mediterranean world, from Greece to Mesopotamia. My speculation: John's readers may have pictured the new Jerusalem as large enough to cover all their known world, so the holy of holies becomes as large as the empire it replaces.`, r:'1 Kings 6:20; Ezekiel 48:16', top:'new-jerusalem'});
N('21:19','precious stones of every kind','echo',{t:'Jewels of the high priest and of Zion', p:'The high priest’s breastplate, and Isaiah’s jeweled city.', b:`Exodus 28:17–21 has twelve stones engraved with the names of the tribes. Isaiah 54:11–12: "lay your foundations with lapis lazuli... your gates of sparkling stones." Tobit 13:16–17 (Scripture in the Catholic and Orthodox Old Testament) also imagines Jerusalem rebuilt with sapphire, emerald and precious stone. Some of the Greek stone names are uncertain, so English Bibles differ (the third stone, <i>chalkēdōn</i>, is "chalcedony" here; some versions say "agate").`, r:'Exodus 28:17–21; Isaiah 54:11–12; Ezekiel 28:13; Tobit 13:16–17', top:'gems'});
N('21:21','a single pearl','spec',{t:'Pearl gates and the pearl of great price', p:'Jesus told of a merchant who sold everything for one pearl.', b:`Pearls were the most valuable gems in the Roman world. In Matthew 13:45–46 the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant who sold everything to buy one priceless pearl. My suggestion: John may want readers to recall that parable, especially after the merchants' lament over Babylon's pearls (18:12, 16). Babylon's merchants lose their pearls; the city of God is entered through them. (The "pearly gates" with Saint Peter as gatekeeper is later folklore.)`, r:'Matthew 13:45–46; 18:12; 18:16', top:'gems'});
N('21:22','I did not see a temple in it','symbol',{t:'No temple', p:'The whole city is the Most Holy Place.', b:`The city is as tall as it is long and wide (21:16). Most readers picture a cube, like the Most Holy Place (1 Kings 6:20), though some picture a pyramid. Only the high priest entered it, once a year. Now everyone lives in God's presence, and "they will see his face" (22:4), which even Moses could not (Exodus 33:20).`, r:'1 Kings 6:20; Exodus 33:20; 22:4; John 2:19–21', top:'temple'});
N('21:24','the kings of the earth bring their glory into it','spec',{t:'The kings of the earth come back', p:'The same kings who served Babylon now bring their glory to the city.', b:`"The kings of the earth" committed sin with Babylon (17:2; 18:9) and fought the Lamb (19:19). Now they bring their glory into the new Jerusalem, as in Isaiah 60:3–11. Many scholars (Bauckham, Caird) see a note of hope for the nations here. My own reading is cautious: the text gives no timeline, but its final picture of "the nations" is of healing (22:2), not destruction.`, r:'Isaiah 60:3–11; 17:2; 19:19; 22:2', top:'new-jerusalem', s:['bauckham-theology']});

/* ---- chapter 22 ---- */
N('22:1','a river of the water of life','echo',{t:'The river from the temple', p:'Ezekiel 47: a river flows from the temple and heals everything.', b:`Ezekiel 47:1–12: water flows from under the temple, growing deeper, making salt water fresh, with fruit trees on both banks whose leaves heal. Zechariah 14:8 and Psalm 46:4 ("A river! Its channels gladden the city of God") join in. Genesis 2:10 has a river flowing out of Eden.`, r:'Ezekiel 47:1–12; Zechariah 14:8; Psalm 46:4; Genesis 2:10; John 7:37–38', top:'tree-of-life'});
N('22:2','healing the nations','spec',{t:'Why does anyone need healing?', p:'In a city with no more pain, the leaves are still "for healing the nations."', b:`If there is no more pain (21:4), why healing? Some read it as a picture of fullness of life, others as a continuing process. My speculation: the leaves answer the curse of Babel and of the nations' long war against God. The nations arrive in the city with histories that need healing, and the tree from Eden provides it.`, r:'Ezekiel 47:12; 21:4; Genesis 11:9; Genesis 3:22', top:'tree-of-life'});
N('22:3','Nothing will be under a curse any longer','echo',{t:'The curse lifted', p:'John quotes Zechariah 14:11, and the story goes back to Genesis 3.', b:`The words come almost exactly from Zechariah 14:11, which in Hebrew says there will never again be a <i>ḥerem</i>, a ban of destruction, on Jerusalem, and that the city will live in safety. The Greek Old Testament there uses <i>anathema</i>; Revelation's <i>katathema</i> is its close cousin. Behind it stands the first curse: "the ground is cursed on your account" (Genesis 3:17). The Bible's story ends where it began, in a garden, with the curse removed.`, r:'Zechariah 14:11; Genesis 3:17; Galatians 3:13', top:'new-creation'});
N('22:4','They will see his face','echo',{t:'The face of God', p:'Moses could not see God’s face and live. Here everyone does.', b:`Exodus 33:20: "You cannot see my face, for no human being can see me and stay alive." Psalm 17:15: "I, in the right, will look upon your face." Jesus: "Blessed are those whose hearts are pure, because they will see God" (Matthew 5:8). The name on their foreheads recalls the high priest's gold plate (Exodus 28:36–38).`, r:'Exodus 33:20; Psalm 17:15; Matthew 5:8; 1 John 3:2; Exodus 28:36–38'});
N('22:10','Do not seal up the words','echo',{t:'Unseal it', p:'Daniel was told to seal his book. John is told not to.', b:`In Daniel 12:4 the angel tells Daniel to close up the words and seal the book until the time of the end. John is told the opposite, "because the time is near." Preterists stress that the nearness was for the first readers. Others see "the time of the end" as having begun with Christ (Hebrews 1:2; 1 John 2:18).`, r:'Daniel 12:4; Daniel 12:9; Hebrews 1:2; 1 John 2:18', top:'soon'});
N('22:11','Let the wrongdoer go on doing wrong','echo',{t:'Let them go on', p:'Daniel 12:10: at the end, the wicked keep on acting wickedly.', b:`Daniel 12:10 says that at the end many will be purified and made white, but the wicked will go on acting wickedly, and none of them will understand. Revelation says the same with a warning edge: the time is near, so the choices people are making now will set. It is not a command to keep sinning but a statement that the hour for changing course is short.`, r:'Daniel 12:10; Daniel 12:4; 22:10; Ezekiel 3:27'});
N('22:12','my reward is with me','echo',{t:'He brings his reward', p:'Isaiah said the Lord GOD would come bringing his reward. Jesus says it of himself.', b:`Isaiah 40:10: "Look, the Lord GOD comes in strength... Look, he brings his reward with him." Isaiah 62:11 repeats it for Daughter Zion. Jesus now speaks Isaiah's words about the Lord GOD as his own, and the next verse gives him God's own title, "the Alpha and the Omega."`, r:'Isaiah 40:10; Isaiah 62:11; 22:13; 1:8; Matthew 16:27'});
N('22:15','the dogs','history',{t:'"The dogs"', p:'A harsh word for people outside the covenant, used by Jews and by Paul.', b:`Dogs in the ancient Near East were scavengers, not pets. The word was an insult for the unclean or for pagans, and Deuteronomy 23:18 may use it for male temple prostitutes. Paul uses it for false teachers (Philippians 3:2). The list matches 21:8.`, r:'Deuteronomy 23:18; Philippians 3:2; 21:8; Matthew 7:6'});
N('22:16','the bright Morning Star','echo',{t:'The Root and the Star', p:'Isaiah’s root of Jesse and Balaam’s star from Jacob, together.', b:`"The Root of David" comes from Isaiah 11:1, 10 (see 5:5). "A star will march out of Jacob" is Balaam's prophecy (Numbers 24:17). The morning star was promised to Thyatira (2:28); now Jesus says he is that star.`, r:'Isaiah 11:1; Isaiah 11:10; Numbers 24:17; 2:28; 2 Peter 1:19'});
N('22:17','The Spirit and the bride say, "Come!"','spec',{t:'Who is being called to come?', p:'"Come!" is said three times: to Jesus, to the hearer, to the thirsty.', b:`The first "Come!" may be the church calling Jesus to return (22:20). The second invites each hearer to join that prayer. The third invites the thirsty to come and drink. My reading: the book ends by turning its four horsemen's "Come!" (6:1, 3, 5, 7), which summoned disasters, into a welcome. The same word that released judgment now opens the door to the water of life.`, r:'6:1; 6:3; 6:5; 6:7; Isaiah 55:1; John 7:37; 22:20'});
N('22:18','if anyone puts anything onto them','echo',{t:'Do not add, do not take away', p:'Moses gave the same warning for the law. It covers this book, not yet the whole Bible.', b:`Deuteronomy 4:2: "Do not add anything to the word I am commanding you, and do not take anything away from it" (also Deuteronomy 12:32). The Greek keeps a play on words: whoever "puts on" more words, God will "put on" him the plagues. Many readers take the warning as sealing the whole Bible, but it speaks of "this book," the prophecy John wrote, at a time when the New Testament had not yet been gathered into one volume. See the chapter page.`, r:'Deuteronomy 4:2; Deuteronomy 12:32; Proverbs 30:6; 22:19', top:'canon'});
N('22:20','Come, Lord Jesus','word',{t:'Maranatha', greek:'ἔρχου κύριε Ἰησοῦ (erchou kyrie Iēsou)', p:'The Greek form of the oldest Christian prayer, "Our Lord, come!"', b:`Paul kept the Aramaic <i>Maranatha</i> in 1 Corinthians 16:22. The Didache (10:6) uses it in a communion prayer. The book that began "the time is near" ends with the church praying for the coming of the one it worships.`, r:'1 Corinthians 16:22; 1:3; 22:7', top:'maranatha'});
N('13:12','the first beast','word',{w:'antichrist', p:'The word "antichrist" never appears in Revelation.', top:'antichrist'});
N('20:4','They came to life and ruled with Christ for a thousand years','word',{w:'millennium', p:'The whole doctrine of the millennium rests on this one passage.', top:'millennium'});
